   "response_text": "All green. Bug squashed. \ud83d\udc36\n\n## Cause\n\nIn `src/click/parser.py`, the `_process_opts` method split the `--name=value` argument like this:\n\n```python\nlong_opt, explicit_value = arg.split(\"=\")[:2]\n```\n\n`str.split(\"=\")` with **no maxsplit** splits on *every* equals sign, and slicing `[:2]` just keeps the first two pieces. So `--name=a=b=c` \u2192 `[\"--name\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]` \u2192 `(\"--name\", \"a\")` \u2014 everything from the second `=` onward got tossed in the bin.\n\n## Fix\n\nSplit only on the first equals sign using `maxsplit=1`:\n\n```python\nlong_opt, explicit_value = arg.split(\"=\", 1)\n```\n\nNow `--name=a=b=c` \u2192 `(\"--name\", \"a=b=c\")`, so the entire remainder after the first `=` becomes the value.\n\n## Verification\n- `--name=a=b=c` \u2192 `'a=b=c'` \u2705 (was `'a'`)\n- `--name a=b=c` \u2192 `'a=b=c'` (separate-arg form, unchanged)\n- `--name=` \u2192 `''` (empty value, unchanged)\n- Full test suite: `588 passed, 22 skipped, 1 xfailed` \u2014 no regressions.\n\nOnly the library source file `src/click/parser.py` was touched (one-line change), and no test files were added, edited, or deleted.\n\nDONE",
